# aclfile — one station, one credential, one topic prefix.
#
# The rule this file exists to enforce: **a station can publish as itself and as
# nothing else.** Without it, one logger's credentials — sitting in a box on a
# pole, reachable by anyone with a screwdriver and a serial cable — can publish
# readings for every site in the network, and MegaNet will store them, because
# the bridge trusts what the broker delivers. Per-station credentials with no
# ACL are per-station credentials in name only.
#
# Same reasoning as #B5's per-station HTTP tokens, and the same shape: the
# credential names the station, the scope is a prefix, and revoking one station
# never touches another.
#
# Managed brokers express this in their own console rather than in a file —
# HiveMQ Cloud and EMQX both have per-credential topic permissions — but the
# rules are the same three lines. Write them there.

# ── The bridge ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Reads everything under the scheme, writes nothing. A bridge that can publish
# is a bridge that can fabricate a reading, and it has no reason to.
user meganet-bridge
topic read meganet/v1/#

# ── A station ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One block per station, generated rather than hand-written once there are more
# than a handful of them:
#
#   for id in $(psql -tAc "select id from meganet.station where enabled"); do
#     printf 'user station-%s\ntopic write meganet/v1/%s/#\n\n' "$id" "$id"
#   done > /etc/mosquitto/aclfile
#
# `write`, not `readwrite`: a logger has nothing to read here. If a future
# ticket adds commands to stations — a request to re-send yesterday, a
# configuration change — that is a second topic branch and a second, explicit
# `topic read` line, not a widening of this one.
user station-loudoun_br_al
topic write meganet/v1/loudoun_br_al/#

user station-abercorn_al
topic write meganet/v1/abercorn_al/#

# ── Nothing else ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Mosquitto denies by default once an ACL file is in place, so there is no
# catch-all to remove. $SYS is left unreadable on purpose: broker internals are
# not a station's business, and the bridge does not read them either.
